<worksheet xml:id="ws-semisimple-algebras">
    <title>Exercises related to semisimple algebras</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            Most of the exercises are taken from <xref ref="bib-Pierce"/>. We assume that <m>R</m> is a commutative ring with unity, and <m>A</m> is an <m>R</m>-algebra.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>A</m> be commutative <m>R</m>-algebra. Show that <m>A</m> is semisimple if and only if <m>A</m> is a finite direct produt of fields.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Show that <m>M_n(A)</m> is right Artinian (resp., Noetherian) if and only if <m>A</m> is right Artinian (resp., Noetherian). Deduce that <m>M_n(R)</m> is not semisimple if <m>R</m> is an integral domain which is not a field.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
            <p>
                Show the following bijection:
                <md>
                    <mrow> \left\{\text{right ideals of }M_n(A)\right\}\amp \longleftrightarrow\left\{\text{submodules of }E_{11}M_n(A)\right\}</mrow>
                    <mrow>\mathcal{I} \amp\mapsto E_{11}\mathcal{I} </mrow>
                    <mrow>\sum_{i=1}^nE_{i1}N \amp\leftarrow N </mrow>
                </md>
                
            </p>
        </hint>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>D</m> be a finite-dimensional division algebra over a field <m>F</m>. Show that <m>M_n(D)</m> is semisimple algebra.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Show that the group algebra <m>\R C_2</m> is semisimple, where <m>C_2</m> is the cyclic group of order 2.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
            <p>
                See <xref ref="eg-group-algebra-cyclic-2"/>.
            </p>
        </hint>
    </exercise>
</worksheet>